add('ai-readiness','AI readiness for companies','Understand organizational, employee and leadership AI readiness, and plan an evidence-based assessment before scaling AI.',f'''
<p class="answer">AI readiness is an organization’s ability to introduce and use AI responsibly in a specific work context. It combines useful applications, employee capabilities, leadership decisions, reliable data, operational support and governance.</p>
<p>Readiness is specific to a task. A company may be ready to test an internal drafting assistant while remaining unprepared to automate a consequential decision. A single enthusiasm score cannot establish either conclusion.</p>
<h2>What companies need to assess</h2><dl><dt>Organizational readiness</dt><dd>Clear use cases, accountable owners, approved tools, data access and measurable success criteria.</dd><dt>Employee readiness</dt><dd>Task knowledge, practical AI skills, time to learn, confidence to question outputs and a route to request help.</dd><dt>Leadership readiness</dt><dd>Explicit priorities, resourcing, escalation rules and willingness to stop an unsafe or ineffective pilot.</dd></dl>
<h2>From readiness to a practical decision</h2><p>Start with one workflow. Record what people do today, the data involved and how quality is checked. Ask employees and leaders separately where they expect value or difficulty. Test a small task with representative users and compare the result with the baseline. Decide what must change before expansion.</p>
<h2>Where NeuroBusiness fits</h2><p>NeuroBusiness currently offers business workstyle diagnostics and Team Intelligence. These can support conversations about collaboration and leadership. They do not by themselves measure AI skills, certify organizational AI readiness or establish technical security. AI-specific assessment scope and availability must be agreed through an inquiry.</p>
<p>{link('/ai-readiness-assessment','Explore the proposed AI readiness assessment approach')} or read {link('/knowledge/how-to-measure-ai-readiness','how to measure readiness')}.</p>
<h2>Evidence and limits</h2><p>{NIST} provides a voluntary risk-management reference. Our readiness structure is an editorial working framework, not a NIST certification or an independently validated NeuroBusiness instrument.</p>''',cta=CONTACT)
add('ai-readiness-assessment','AI readiness assessment for companies','A transparent approach to assessing AI adoption conditions: scope, interviews, practical tasks, evidence and pilot decisions.',f'''
<p class="answer">An AI readiness assessment is a structured review of the conditions a company needs to use AI effectively and responsibly. It should identify strengths, missing evidence and barriers for a defined use case, then connect each finding to an action and an owner.</p>
<p class="status">Availability: inquiry and scope discussion. A standalone NeuroBusiness AI readiness test, automated score and validated company benchmark are not currently documented as released products.</p>
<h2>Proposed assessment process</h2><ol><li><strong>Scope:</strong> agree a workflow, affected roles, business outcome and prohibited uses.</li><li><strong>Evidence:</strong> combine interviews, a short employee questionnaire, process documentation and practical task observations.</li><li><strong>Interpretation:</strong> distinguish skill gaps from missing access, unclear rules, workload and legitimate concerns.</li><li><strong>Decision:</strong> document conditions for a limited pilot, remediation work or a pause.</li><li><strong>Review:</strong> repeat the practical checks after training and process changes.</li></ol>
<h2>What a useful deliverable contains</h2><p>A use-case summary; readiness findings by role; an evidence register; adoption barriers; unresolved risks; a prioritized action plan; and pilot measures. The proposed format uses descriptive findings rather than a false precision score. A survey average can conceal a team with no approved tool or no time to learn.</p>
<h2>AI readiness test versus business diagnostic</h2><p>The existing NeuroBusiness 50-question diagnostic explores work patterns. The free Quick-Scan is an orientation to those patterns. Neither is presented here as an AI competency test. Companies should supplement workstyle conversations with task-based AI evaluation and technical review.</p>
<h2>For companies in Germany</h2><p>Discuss working language, employee participation, confidentiality and intended decisions before collecting responses. This page does not establish legal compliance or imply a Berlin office. Contact NeuroBusiness to clarify delivery arrangements for your team.</p>
<h2>Method and next step</h2><p>{link('/methodology','Read the methodology and validation limits')}. For practical measurement, see {link('/knowledge/how-to-measure-ai-readiness','the readiness measurement guide')}. Share your use case, approximate team size and desired decision when contacting us; do not send employee reports or sensitive data by email.</p>''',cta=CONTACT)
add('ai-adoption','Human-centered AI adoption and consulting','Plan AI adoption around real work, employee participation, leadership support and measurable pilot outcomes.',f'''
<p class="answer">AI adoption is the process of integrating AI into everyday work with the skills, responsibilities and checks needed to use it well. Human-centered adoption involves the people doing the work in choosing, testing and improving the system.</p>
<h2>What AI adoption consulting should address</h2><p>A useful engagement links a business problem to workflow design, practical training and a decision about scale. It should identify who will review AI outputs, who can approve access, how employees can report problems and what evidence would justify changing course.</p>
<h2>A working sequence for a company pilot</h2><ol><li>Choose a task with a clear owner and reviewable output.</li><li>Map the current workflow and baseline quality, effort and error rates.</li><li>Ask affected employees what makes the task difficult and what support is missing.</li><li>Introduce an approved tool with example tasks and explicit review rules.</li><li>Compare performance and experience with the baseline, then revise or stop.</li></ol>
<h2>Support people without labeling them</h2><p>Low use can reflect poor task fit, lack of time, tool friction or well-founded concern. Treat these as questions to investigate. A workstyle profile must not become a label for resistance or a proxy for employability.</p>
<h2>NeuroBusiness contribution and current scope</h2><p>Team Intelligence and business diagnostics provide a starting point for discussing roles and communication. AI adoption consulting and a company pilot can be discussed through an inquiry; scope, deliverables and availability require explicit agreement. No automated adoption platform or guaranteed productivity result is offered on this page.</p>
<h2>Evidence-informed implementation</h2><p>{OECD} discusses AI’s implications for work, skills and workplace organization. It does not validate NeuroBusiness products. Read {link('/knowledge/ai-adoption-barriers','how to identify adoption barriers')} and {link('/human-ai-transformation','how to organize human-centered transformation')}.</p>''',cta=('/contact?topic=ai-adoption','Discuss your company pilot'))
add('human-ai-transformation','Human AI transformation: work, leadership and governance','A practical approach to AI transformation that connects work design, human factors, leadership and governance.',f'''
<p class="answer">Human-centered AI transformation is a change in how an organization works with AI that explicitly considers people’s roles, capabilities, decision rights and experience. Tool deployment is one part of that change.</p>
<h2>Readiness, adoption and transformation</h2><p>{link('/ai-readiness','Readiness')} asks whether the conditions are in place. {link('/ai-adoption','Adoption')} concerns use in daily work. Transformation changes workflows, responsibilities and the organization’s operating choices over time. The terms should not be treated as interchangeable product promises.</p>
<h2>Human factors to examine</h2><p>Look for unclear accountability, misplaced trust in outputs, limited learning time, conflicting incentives and uncertainty about job changes. Interview people close to the workflow before selecting an intervention. A training session will not repair an inaccessible system or an incentive to hide errors.</p>
<h2>A transformation review</h2><ol><li>Map which tasks change and who remains accountable.</li><li>Identify skills, access and support required for each role.</li><li>Establish a review path for errors and disputed outputs.</li><li>Agree measures of quality and workload as well as speed.</li><li>Review the effects with employees before expanding.</li></ol>
<h2>Evidence and boundaries</h2><p>{NIST} offers a risk-management structure. {link('/knowledge/why-ai-transformations-fail','Our failure analysis guide')} applies a practical diagnostic approach without asserting a universal failure rate. NeuroBusiness’s own models are described separately in {link('/methodology','Methodology')}.</p>''',cta=('/contact?topic=transformation','Discuss a transformation use case'))
add('team-intelligence','Team Intelligence and the NeuroBusiness Team Map','Understand team work patterns, clarify contributions and connect team discussions to practical decisions.', '''
<p class="answer">NeuroBusiness Team Intelligence uses business workstyle assessments to support conversations about team roles, communication and collective perspectives. It is a reflection and development approach, not a ranking of people’s value or ability.</p>
<h2>Existing Team Scan offer</h2><p>The current team offer describes confidential individual participation, an aggregated team profile, a Team Intelligence Report, a contribution matrix and communication or task-briefing guides. See the <a href="/teams">Team Scan offer and inquiry flow</a> for scope and terms.</p>
<h2>What “Team Map” means here</h2><p>Team Map is a descriptive name for making the team’s working patterns and contributions visible. This page does not claim that a separate product under that name has been released. The implemented offer is Team Intelligence. Use the map to formulate questions, then check them against actual work.</p>
<h2>Example: a delayed decision</h2><p>Record the decision, required evidence, decision owner and people affected. Ask whether the delay reflects missing information, unclear authority or incompatible expectations. Use the profile as a conversation aid, then agree a concrete decision rule and review it after use.</p>
<h2>Boundaries and AI adoption</h2><p>Do not infer AI competence from a workstyle type. Combine team reflection with task-based evidence, access checks and an <a href="/ai-readiness-assessment">AI readiness assessment plan</a>. Aggregate findings can still be sensitive in small teams; agree who can see what before participation.</p>
<p>For the proprietary model and evidence status, read <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a>. Related contexts: <a href="/leadership-intelligence">leadership</a> and <a href="/founder-intelligence">founder teams</a>.</p>''',kind='Service',cta=('/teams','Explore Team Scan and request a Team Map discussion'))
add('leadership-intelligence','Leadership Intelligence and AI readiness','Use structured leadership reflection to clarify decisions, delegation and the conditions for responsible AI adoption.', '''
<p class="answer">Leadership Intelligence at NeuroBusiness describes the application of workstyle reflection to leadership decisions, communication and delegation. It does not denote a separately validated leadership ability score.</p>
<h2>Leadership questions worth assessing</h2><p>Who decides when AI may be used? Who reviews its output? Which decisions require a person’s approval? How can employees raise concerns without being treated as resistant? Answer these with examples from recent work, rather than general confidence ratings.</p>
<h2>From assessment to a leadership guide</h2><p>A useful guide records decision responsibilities, briefing preferences, feedback routines and situations requiring escalation. The existing team offer includes communication and task-briefing support. A separately named Leadership Guide is not documented here as a released standalone product.</p>
<h2>Delegation requires context</h2><p>For each task, record the owner, reviewer, permitted AI assistance, required input and stop conditions. This is a proposed working template, not an automated delegation decision or a validated instrument. Review the agreement with the people doing the task.</p>
<h2>Current next step</h2><p>Discuss leadership assessment needs through the existing Team Scan inquiry. Confirm whether individual reflection, team work or a facilitated session is appropriate before booking. Read <a href="/knowledge/leadership-and-ai-adoption">how leadership affects AI adoption</a> and the <a href="/methodology">methodology limits</a>.</p>''',cta=('/contact?topic=leadership','Discuss leadership assessment needs'))
add('founder-intelligence','Founder Intelligence and founder team assessment','Clarify founder roles, decisions and working expectations using business diagnostics and structured team reflection.', '''
<p class="answer">Founder team assessment is a structured discussion of how co-founders work together: their responsibilities, decision habits, communication and expectations under pressure. NeuroBusiness applies business diagnostics as one input to that discussion.</p>
<h2>A different decision context</h2><p>Founder teams often combine ownership, strategy and operational work in the same relationships. An assessment should distinguish these roles. A disagreement about investment authority is not necessarily a personality conflict.</p>
<h2>A practical founder conversation</h2><ol><li>List recurring strategic and operational decisions.</li><li>Assign a decision owner and agree what requires joint approval.</li><li>Identify missing perspectives without labeling a founder as unsuitable.</li><li>Record how disagreement is raised and when outside facilitation is useful.</li><li>Review the arrangement against actual decisions after a trial period.</li></ol>
<h2>Use the right scope</h2><p>The existing <a href="/teams">Team Scan</a> is the relevant inquiry route. Its advertised aggregation threshold may not suit a two-person founder team; agree a suitable facilitated format rather than assuming a dashboard will be available. Individual reports require appropriate permission before sharing.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2><p>Workstyle patterns do not predict venture success, investment returns or founder compatibility. NeuroBusiness Types are model-based profile hypotheses. See <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a> and <a href="/leadership-intelligence">Leadership Intelligence</a>.</p>''',cta=('/contact?topic=founder-team','Discuss your founder team'))
add('enterprise-ai','Enterprise AI: human context and product direction','NeuroBusiness enterprise AI direction, with current capabilities separated from development work and planned integrations.', '''
<p class="answer">The NeuroBusiness enterprise direction is a human context layer: helping organizations connect AI use with work patterns, team context and leadership decisions. The broader integration layer is a product vision, not a currently available enterprise connector platform.</p>
<h2>Available now</h2><p>The existing website offers business diagnostics, individual reports, Team Intelligence and an AI Companion. Access depends on the relevant offer and account permissions. Review <a href="/solo">individual offers</a> and <a href="/teams">team offers</a> for details. Existing functionality is not evidence that enterprise integrations have shipped.</p>
<h2>In development</h2><p>NBIF methodology refinement and validation preparation are documented development work. The AI readiness approach on this site is a proposed assessment structure. A separate enterprise human context layer has no verified release milestone published here.</p>
<h2>Planned</h2><p>The long-term concept connects enterprise systems, AI assistants, organizational context, human readiness, team structure and leadership context. Any implementation would need scoped permissions, auditable access, data minimization and a way to correct or withdraw context.</p>
<h2>An illustrative future workflow</h2><p>An assistant preparing a team meeting could receive authorized, relevant team-level context and the purpose of the meeting. It should not receive raw individual diagnostic responses by default. That scenario illustrates the intended design direction; it is not a demonstration of a released integration.</p>
<h2>Integration status</h2><p>No implemented Model Context Protocol (MCP) server or connector is established by this repository audit. No MCP integration, enterprise SSO, permission synchronization or production service-level commitment is promised here.</p>
<p>Discuss a bounded pilot and its data requirements through <a href="/contact">Contact</a>. Read <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a> before using diagnostic context in decisions.</p>''',cta=('/contact?topic=enterprise-ai','Discuss an enterprise use case'))
add('methodology','NeuroBusiness methodology and research status','NBIF, NeuroBusiness Types and proposed AI readiness methods: definitions, evidence boundaries and validation status.', '''
<p class="answer">The NeuroBusiness Intelligence Framework (NBIF) is a proprietary business workstyle model. The repository documents NBIF 1.1 Cognitive Pilot as a 50-question instrument across five dimensions. Published independent psychometric validation is not established by the documentation reviewed for this page.</p>
<h2>Four different kinds of statements</h2><dl><dt>Research evidence</dt><dd>External studies support only the claims they actually investigate. They do not automatically validate NBIF.</dd><dt>NeuroBusiness model</dt><dd>A proprietary structure used to organize self-reported work patterns and practical reflection.</dd><dt>Hypothesis</dt><dd>An interpretation to check against experience and task evidence, rather than a proven fact about a person.</dd><dt>Product function</dt><dd>A behavior implemented in software or explicitly included in an agreed service. A concept name alone does not establish availability.</dd></dl>
<h2 id="nbif">NBIF 1.1 Cognitive Pilot</h2><p>The five documented dimensions concern energy and visibility; cognition and decision-making; motivation; implementation and scaling; and stress regulation and self-leadership. Responses support a workstyle profile. They do not measure brain structure, establish a clinical diagnosis or objectively certify competence.</p>
<h2 id="types">NeuroBusiness Types</h2><p>Strategist, Visionary, Builder, Connector and High Performer are interpretive profile labels. They are not empirically established discrete classes. The repository contains differing H/G labels for High Performer; interpretation should refer to the instrument and scoring version, not only the letter. The internal audit also identifies overlap between performance-related interpretation and strain indicators.</p>
<h2 id="ai-readiness">AI Readiness Framework</h2><p>The proposed framework separates use-case clarity, people and skills, leadership and work design, data and tools, and governance. Use an evidence register with four descriptive states: not examined, gap identified, ready for a bounded pilot, and demonstrated in that pilot. These are working categories, not validated scales or population norms. NBIF scores must not be converted into an AI readiness score.</p>
<h2 id="team-map">Team Map</h2><p>A descriptive view of team work patterns and contributions. The existing Team Intelligence offer provides an aggregated team profile and contribution matrix. A separate released artifact named Team Map has not been verified.</p>
<h2 id="leadership-guide">Leadership Guide</h2><p>A proposed structure for decision rights, briefing expectations, feedback and escalation. Existing communication and task-briefing guides are described in the team offer; a standalone product under this exact name is not verified.</p>
<h2 id="delegation-matrix">Delegation Matrix</h2><p>A proposed task-owner-reviewer table with permitted AI use and stop conditions. It is a working method, not an automated assignment engine. Do not infer delegation authority from a profile label.</p>
<h2 id="conflict-snapshot">Conflict Snapshot</h2><p>A proposed record separating the observable disagreement, each person’s interpretation, the work context and an agreed next step. No separately released diagnostic measure with this name was found. It must not be used to diagnose individuals or predict conflict.</p>
<h2>What remains to be established</h2><p>The internal July 2026 audit recommends cognitive pretests, item analysis, reliability work, factor analysis and comparison with relevant constructs. It is a plan, not evidence that those studies have been completed. Publish sample definitions, methods, scoring versions, limitations and results before making validation claims.</p>
<h2>Appropriate use</h2><p>Use reports as a basis for reflection and voluntary development. Do not use them alone for hiring, promotion, exclusion, medical decisions or AI access eligibility. Reassess interpretations when role, workload or context changes.</p>
<p>Explore the <a href="/research">research and evidence register</a>, <a href="/founder">founder information</a> and <a href="/ai-readiness-assessment">assessment scope</a>.</p>''')
add('research','Research and evidence behind our AI guidance','Primary references, their relevance and their limits, with a clear distinction from NeuroBusiness product validation.',f'''
<p class="answer">NeuroBusiness uses external research as context for practical guidance. External evidence about AI at work does not constitute validation of the NeuroBusiness Intelligence Framework or its proposed AI readiness framework.</p>
<h2>Risk management</h2><p>{NIST} is a voluntary framework for managing AI risks. It provides a useful reference for identifying responsibilities and review processes. It is not an assessment accreditation or an endorsement of NeuroBusiness.</p>
<h2>Work and skills</h2><p>{OECD} examines AI in the labour market, including skills and workplace implications. It supports considering work organization alongside technology; it does not establish a universal readiness score.</p>
<h2>Workplace field evidence</h2><p>{NBER} studies an AI assistant in a customer-support setting. Reported effects differ across workers. That setting cannot establish the expected benefit for every company, role or AI tool.</p>
<h2>NeuroBusiness original work</h2><p>The public <a href="/methodology">methodology register</a> documents NBIF and distinguishes proposed concepts from released functions. No peer-reviewed NeuroBusiness validation study, independent benchmark or public research dataset is claimed here.</p>
<h2>Editorial practice</h2><p>We identify the source of external findings, distinguish inference from evidence and avoid turning a study result into a product guarantee. Corrections or questions can be sent through <a href="/contact">Contact</a>. Dates on these pages indicate editorial review, not the completion of a new study.</p>''')
